Thanks for the v0.1.8 update. We re-ran our review and found the icon issue we flagged on May 7 is still present — the published tarball ships the SVGs at the wrong paths, so n8n can't load them at runtime.

**[HIGH] Icon files published to the wrong paths in the npm tarball**

The compiled node references `icon: 'file:icon.svg'`, which n8n resolves relative to the compiled `.js` file. That means n8n expects:
- `dist/nodes/Syphoon/icon.svg` (for the node)
- `dist/credentials/icon.svg` (for the credential)

Inspecting the published `n8n-nodes-syphoon@0.1.8` tarball, the icons are actually shipped at:
- `dist/Syphoon/icon.svg` ← missing the `nodes/` prefix
- `dist/icon.svg` ← missing the `credentials/` prefix

The root cause is in `gulpfile.js`. The current task strips the leading `nodes/` and `credentials/` directories from the destination path because each glob uses its own base. A one-line fix:

```js
const gulp = require('gulp');

gulp.task('build:icons', function () {
  return gulp
    .src('{nodes,credentials}/**/icon.svg', { base: '.' })
    .pipe(gulp.dest('dist'));
});

gulp.task('default', gulp.series('build:icons'));
```

After rebuilding and republishing, please verify the tarball with `npm pack` — it should contain `package/dist/nodes/Syphoon/icon.svg` and `package/dist/credentials/icon.svg`.

For reference: https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/creating-nodes/build/reference/node-base-files/#node-icon

Thank you for the v1.1.0 update — the dynamic agent discovery and configurable base URL are a great improvement. Before we can approve, there is one issue to fix:

**\[HIGH\] WhatsApp template parameters are always sent empty**

In this update, the code that reads user-entered template parameters from the "Parameters" field was commented out and replaced with an empty object. This means all WhatsApp template messages are dispatched with empty parameter arrays regardless of what the user fills in — the UI field appears to work, but the values are silently ignored.

Please restore the parameter-reading code in `nodes/KippsAi/KippsAi.node.ts` in the WhatsApp execute branch:

```ts

const mapped = this.getNodeParameter('mappedParameters', i) as {

 value: Record<string, unknown>;

};

const values = mapped?.value || {};
```

Once this is fixed and republished, please request a new review.

Thank you for submitting the v0.3.0 update for `n8n-nodes-databar`! We found a few issues that need to be resolved before we can approve this update.

**\[HIGH\] Publish with npm provenance**

The package must be published via a GitHub Actions workflow with the `--provenance` flag. This is required for all verified community nodes as of May 1st 2026. Please update your publish workflow to include provenance and republish. See: [https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/creating-nodes/deploy/submit-community-nodes/#submit-your-node-for-verification-by-n8n](<https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/creating-nodes/deploy/submit-community-nodes/#submit-your-node-for-verification-by-n8n>)

**\[HIGH\] Use** `NodeApiError` **for HTTP errors in** `execute()`

In `nodes/Databar/Databar.node.ts` around line 1810, the `catch` block inside the enrichment `run` operation wraps HTTP errors in `NodeOperationError`. Please use `NodeApiError` instead so the HTTP status code and response body are preserved in the n8n error display:

import { ..., NodeApiError, ... } from 'n8n-workflow';

// in the catch block:

throw new NodeApiError(this.getNode(), error as JsonObject);

**\[MEDIUM\] Fix node codex file (**`nodes/Databar/Databar.node.json`**)**

Two fixes needed:

1. Remove the `subcategories` field — it is not a supported codex field and will be ignored.
2. Change the `node` field from `n8n-nodes-base.databar` to `n8n-nodes-databar.databar` to match your npm package name.

**\[MEDIUM\] Handle errors in the** `getWaterfalls` **dropdown loader**

In `nodes/Databar/Databar.node.ts` around line 919, the catch block silently swallows all errors, leaving users with an empty waterfall dropdown and no feedback. Please surface the error as an option, the same way `getEnrichments` already does:

} catch (error) {

const errorMessage = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Failed to load waterfalls';

returnData.push({ name: `Error: ${errorMessage}`, value: '', description: 'Could not load waterfalls. Check your API key and try again.' });

}

**\[MEDIUM\] Remove dead** `requestDefaults` **config**

In `nodes/Databar/Databar.node.ts` around line 195, the `requestDefaults` block (baseURL, Accept/Content-Type headers) is only applied in declarative-style nodes. Since this node uses `execute()` with `httpRequestWithAuthentication`, it is never used. Please remove it.

Once these are fixed and a new version is published with provenance, please let us know and we will take another look!

Thank you for the update to \`n8n-nodes-tanss\`! We found two issues to address before we can approve this version.

\*\*\[MEDIUM\] \`checkItem\` — \`linkTypeId\` and \`linkId\` not sent to the API\*\*

The TANSS check-item endpoint (\`PUT /api/v1/checklists/check\`) requires \`linkTypeId\` and \`linkId\` in the request body (confirmed via the bundled API spec). In v1.1.22 these were part of the \`checkItemFields\` collection and included in the body. This update moved them to standalone UI fields but the handler sends only \`checkItemFields\` as the body — so neither value reaches the API and the operation fails at runtime.

\*\*Fix\*\* in \`nodes/tanss/sub/Checklists.ts\` (\`case 'checkItem'\`): either add \`linkTypeId\` and \`linkId\` back into the \`checkItemFields\` collection definition, or read the outer parameters and merge them into the body before the API call:

const linkTypeId = this.getNodeParameter('linkTypeId', i, 11) as number;

const linkId = this.getNodeParameter('linkId', i, 0) as number;

requestOptions.body = { ...checkItemFields, linkTypeId, linkId };

\*\*\[MEDIUM\] README — no concrete usage example\*\*

The README was significantly reduced in this update and no longer contains any inline usage examples. Verified nodes require at least one end-to-end example (e.g. how to fetch tickets, create a comment, or use the new Project operations). Installation and Credentials sections are well-covered — please add at least one usage example to complete the README.

Please publish a new version once both items are addressed. This should be fixed before the update can be approved.
